# OpenABL kit provenance

abl_signed-<SOC>.elf is NOT committed here - the PKGBUILD fetches it from the
official OpenABL release, pinned by tag + sha256:

  release:  https://github.com/OpenHandheldCollective/OpenABL/releases/tag/v1.2
  asset:    openabl-v1.2.tar.gz (one folder per SoC)
  tarball sha256: 28cb97d1b387f28ddba28ed57fdc7d8b1c2ac2cdcd26af9c672620d41abddd4f
  (the same pin ROCKNIX's openabl package.mk carries, ROCKNIX 73ffb8ee37)
  v1.1 reset the device while starting an Android boot image: BootLinux checked its
  stack canary with the MMU off and could read a stale cached value (OpenABL dad6370,
  issue #29, first in v1.1.1).
  Each SoC's elf is checked against the release's own .sha256 sidecar in prepare(), and the
  tarball's sha256 above pins the elfs and the sidecars together.

OpenABL is the Open Handheld Collective's build of the Qualcomm ABL (edk2
LinuxLoader with one patch plus a boot menu, GPL-3.0-or-later; see the
LICENSES.md it ships, installed under /usr/share/licenses). It boots Boot
Loader Spec entries, extlinux, EFI loaders and, what Steamling uses, an
Android boot image at /KERNEL on a FAT partition (board dtb picked from the
dtbs appended to it). One elf serves every board of a SoC; the release keys
it by SoC. One recipe builds the package for each SoC (STEAMLING_SOC names it).

It replaces the ROCKNIX ABL (github.com/ROCKNIX/abl, v1.2), whose repo and
release downloads were removed in October 2026. ROCKNIX switched to OpenABL
in their distribution repo (71d8932b33 "openabl: introduce package", then
a5d0dd4d57 "openabl: bump to v1.1"). epoch=1 because OpenABL's 1.1 sorts
below the ROCKNIX ABL's 1.2.

ROCKNIX renamed the kit folder to openabl/ at the same time, and ours followed
in October 2026: the old name made the kit look like something to fetch from
ROCKNIX first. steamling-refresh-abl-kit moves backups a device already keeps
in /flash/rocknix_abl over to /flash/openabl, and the kit scripts still look in
/sdcard/rocknix_abl when they aren't run from their own folder.

The three kit scripts started as the ROCKNIX distribution repo's
packages/tools/openabl/sources/ (GPL-2.0 tree): the same dd commands, with
checks added. They find their own folder instead of assuming /sdcard/openabl,
backup_abl.sh never overwrites a backup that's already there, flash_abl.sh
won't run without one, and all three say what they did. README is ours.
Vendored (tiny, change rarely) rather than fetched or synced; diff the dd
lines against the ROCKNIX tree when advancing the sync pin.

Version coupling: the ABL version ROCKNIX pins lives in their
projects/ROCKNIX/packages/tools/openabl/package.mk (PKG_VERSION). Ours (_ablver
in the PKGBUILD) should track it - the ABL defines the boot contract the
profile assumes (FAT label, /KERNEL boot image, board-id dtb select), so a
mismatched pair is the thing to avoid.

The kit's job is FIRST INSTALL from stock Android: the user copies
openabl/ off the SD's boot FAT to Android internal storage, runs
backup_abl.sh (saves the stock abl_a/abl_b next to the scripts - tell users
to copy those .img backups OFF the device; an install-to-internal later
wipes userdata and the backup with it), then flash_abl.sh, both as root.
restore_backup_abl.sh is the road back to a fully stock device.

Once a device runs steamling, ABL UPDATES no longer need Android: steamling-update-abl
(steamling-bsp-common, reachable from Steamling Tools) flashes this same elf onto
abl_a/abl_b from the running system. It is user-run only - no alpm hook, nothing
at boot or update time, unlike ROCKNIX's updateabl which their bootloader
update.sh calls on every OS update. Its backup goes to /flash/openabl with
the kit's own filenames and is never overwritten, so a stock backup made the
Android way survives and restore_backup_abl.sh keeps working.
